{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"Split the outer conjunction and treat each `Iff` as an equivalence between two independently true facts; no mathematical implication between the paired statements is needed.\n\n- For the first `Iff`:\n  - Right adjoints are unique: for each `y`, both `g₁ y` and `g₂ y` are least upper bounds of `{x | f x ≤ y}`. Antisymmetry in `α`, followed by function extensionality, gives `g₁ = g₂`. This is exactly `IsOrderRightAdjoint.unique`.\n  - To obtain `CountableSupClosed s`, represent every nonempty countable subset of `s` by a sequence indexed by `ℕ` (repeating an element when necessary). Its least upper bound is the corresponding `iSup`, so `hs` supplies membership. This is packaged by `CountableSupClosed.of_iSup_mem`.\n\n- For the second `Iff`:\n  - `countableSupClosure s` has exactly the same upper bounds as `s`, hence the same least upper bounds. Use `isLUB_countableSupClosure`.\n  - In a coframe, finite sup distributes over arbitrary inf. Thus, if two elements of `countableInfClosure s` are represented by countable infima of families in `s`, their supremum is the countable infimum of the pairwise suprema; these lie in `s` by `hs`. This is the lemma `SupClosed.countableInfClosure`.\n\nConsequently, after splitting each `Iff`, each direction can simply return the appropriate unconditional theorem, ignoring its premise. The key assumptions are antisymmetry for uniqueness and the coframe distributive law for preservation of sup-closure. [Mathlib’s countable-closure API](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Order/CountableSupClosed.html) contains the latter three closure lemmas."}}
